include g474.tdc
diagram e-fast "ISR → thread, fast mode (20 %): the slice reset does not reprogram SysTick"
letter "E fast"
new
num value "1269 cycles"
num share "20 %"
num path "wrapped ISR, k_sem, defaults"
num arch "PendSV (USE_SWITCH=n)"
what "The ISR gives a semaphore, and the woken thread runs after PendSV switches to it. With time slicing, the give also resets the slice timeout for the woken thread, inside the ISR. In this mode the main thread's poll timeout (every 100 ms) expires before the new slice timeout (20 ms). This is true for 20 % of the poll period. So the slice timeout is not the first timeout, and SysTick keeps its value. The dashed cursor shows where the slow mode ends (card E slow)."
evidence "Fast-mode test: fast share 20.15 % (stimulus 100..500 µs), 20.25 % (100..200 µs), 20.13 % (400..500 µs), 20.06 % (fixed 300 µs), 1.99 % with a 1000 ms poll. Only the poll period changes the share, as the prediction of 2026-10-25 said."
evidence "With 16 load timers the slice timeout is almost never the first timeout: almost all wakes are fast (slow share 0.3 %), p50 1413."
evidence "TIMESLICING=n: one mode at 694 cycles (wake-anchors-1m). So time slicing costs about 575 cycles also without the reprogram."
src "wake-isr-1m/20261029T064014Z, wake-anchors-1m/20261029T014607Z, wake-fast-mode/20261029T071156Z, wake-timers-1m/20261029T045125Z"

axis "cycles after the edge" -100..1750

idle sleep -100..4 "WFI"
mask -100..9 "PRIMASK"
mask 155..911 "BASEPRI" : The give and the slice reset run with the scheduler lock and the timeout lock held: sem_lock (kernel/sem.c:119), the scheduler lock in z_sched_wake() (kernel/include/ksched.h:293-310), timeout_lock in z_add_timeout() (kernel/timeout.c:191).
mask 941..971? "BASEPRI" : z_arm_pendsv sets BASEPRI at swap_helper.S:123 and restores the BASEPRI of the woken thread (0) at swap_helper.S:270, before the MPU reprogram (bl z_arm_configure_dynamic_mpu_regions, :305). The length is not measured: an estimate from the number of instructions.
at 0
hw 60 "entry" : Pin, EXTI, NVIC, wake-up, exception entry, _isr_wrapper. Cold cache.
dev 26 : The ISR reads TIM2->CNT: time stamp 1 (IRQ p50 86).
dev 250 "k_sem_give()" : The ISR gives the semaphore. The kernel makes the waiting thread ready. update_cache() selects it as the next thread (kernel/sched.c:172-176).
dev 575? "slice reset, no reprogram" : update_cache() calls z_time_slice_reset() for the next thread: z_try_abort_timeout() and z_add_timeout() of the slice timeout. The main thread's poll timeout expires first, so the slice timeout is not the first timeout, and the driver does not program SysTick. Time slicing costs about 575 cycles also here (1269 against 694 without slicing). The split between this code and the switch path is not measured.
hw 10 : Tail-chaining into PendSV.
pendsv 378 "switch idle → thread" : z_arm_pendsv: save the idle thread, take the woken thread from the ready queue cache (with BASEPRI), program the MPU stack guard, restore the woken thread. Length: the rest of the 694 cycles without slicing (an estimate).
waker 56 : The thread returns from z_swap() and k_sem_take() and reads TIM2->CNT: time stamp 2.

@t0_1 edge "edge" at 0
@t0_2 stamp "stamp 1" at 86
@t0_3 stamp "stamp 2" at 1355
@t0_4 ref "slow mode" at 1668
span 0..86 "86" !
span 86..1355 "1269, measured" !
